  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nAt noon, temperatures were running within a few degrees of what the \ntemperatures were at noon yesterday. Cooling is expected to start to \nspread inland as onshore flow gradually increases over the next few \ndays. Widespread below average temperatures are expected through \nmuch of this week with highs on Saturday and Sunday running 10 to 20 \ndegrees below average for inland locations. Patchy marine layer low \nclouds and fog may develop tonight into Tuesday morning near the \ncoast. Low cloud and fog coverage is expected to become more \nwidespread for Tuesday night into Wednesday and will remain \npersistent each night and morning through the week. \n\nThe weather becomes unsettled for the second half of the week with \nthe center of a closed low near the Pacific Northwest coast for \nThursday through Sunday. A nearly zonal subtropical jet stream from \nthe central and eastern Pacific into northern Baja will strengthen \nfor Friday and Saturday, possibly transporting a low pressure into \nnorthern Baja or far southern California some time around late \nSaturday into Sunday. There remains a lot of uncertainty in the \ndetails of the forecast this weekend, but there is increasing \nconfidence that the bulk of the precipitation won't arrive until \nSunday. That being said, periods of on and off light showers may \ndevelop as early as Friday or Saturday. Given the uncertainties on \nif the bulk of the moisture will be transported into Southern \nCalifornia or northern Baja, there is quite a range of potential \nrainfall totals. The GFS has been trending wetter, with the bulk of \nthe moisture plume closer to Southern California. On the other \nhand the ECMWF solutions continue to show the bulk of the moisture\nto our south and east. For context, the range of potential \noutcomes for 24 hour precipitation totals Sunday are 1.5 to 2.5 \ninches on the high end and near 0.10 to 0.25 inches on the low \nend. \n\nIn addition to rain, winds are expected to increase over the \nmountains and deserts. There remains uncertainty in the wind speeds, \nwhich will largely be determined by the track of the system. This \nlooks like a warm system, which will keep the snow level near and \nabout 8500 ft. Interested parties should continue to monitor the \nforecast in the coming days due to the moderate to high uncertainty \nin the details.",
